Found four actionable issues.

- [P1] Half-written files can still be claimed during a write race — [watch_folders.py:117](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/watch_folders.py:117)

  Stability is checked before opening the file, but the signature is never rechecked after reading. If a writer resumes between `stat()` and `read()`/`os.replace()`, the service may enqueue partial content and move a file that is still being written. Re-stat after reading and require the signature to remain identical before claiming it.

- [P2] Persisted queues are abandoned after an application restart — [execution.py:138](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/execution.py:138)

  `ExecutionManager` always starts with empty in-memory queues and never loads persisted `pending` or `running` records. After a restart, these entries remain permanently non-terminal, cannot be cancelled because they are absent from `active`, and never execute. Startup should recover pending entries and reconcile interrupted running entries, likely as failed/cancelled or retried under an explicit policy.

- [P2] Valid cron expressions can block scheduling for minutes — [scheduled_runs.py:98](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/scheduled_runs.py:98)

  `next_after()` scans minute by minute for up to six years. An accepted expression such as `0 0 31 2 *` took 10.9 seconds before reporting no fire time; 16 schedules can therefore block the endpoint for nearly three minutes. The same backward scan can stall the single scheduler thread after lengthy downtime, preventing unrelated workflows from firing. Use field-wise calendar advancement or a proven cron iterator.

- [P2] Unseen-notification badges can permanently miss new notifications — [execution.py:318](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/execution.py:318)

  The scheduler emits its terminal event before `dispatch_run_notification()` persists the record. The UI refreshes its badge from that terminal status, so it can query before the notification exists and never retry. Schedule/watch/webhook executions are worse: they generally never become `currentExecution`, so they produce no badge refresh at all. Persist before the terminal signal or add notification-specific events/polling.

